## HOW TO WRITE THE SUBJECT — follow these four steps once, in order

Do not generate candidate subjects and compare them. Use one pass through these steps to produce the subject, then write the body.

**Step 1 — pick the kind.** Take the FIRST row that matches the diff:

| The diff mainly... | kind |
|---|---|
| changes only repo maintenance, tooling, config, or dependencies | chore |
| changes only CI/workflow files | ci |
| changes only build scripts or packaging | build |
| changes only documentation or comments | docs |
| changes only tests | test |
| changes only formatting, with no behavior change | style |
| fixes wrong behavior | fix |
| adds behavior that did not exist | feat |
| restructures code with no behavior change | refactor |
| changes code to make it faster | perf |

**Step 2 — pick the scope.** If one directory or subsystem owns most of the
diff, use its name. Otherwise omit the scope and the parentheses entirely.
When scope is not obvious in one look, omit it.

**Step 3 — write the description.** Say what the change does, in one clause,
in lowercase imperative mood. Describe behavior rather than files; use a precise imperative clause. There is no length limit; stop at its end.

**Step 4 — check the grammar, then stop.** The subject must match exactly:

```
<kind>(<scope>)?!?: <description>
```

- `<kind>` is lowercase and from the table above.
- `<scope>`, if present, is lowercase letters, digits, `/`, `_`, `-` only —
  no dots, no uppercase.
- `!` before the colon marks a breaking change.
- Exactly one space follows the colon.
- `<description>` starts with a lowercase letter or digit.
- Write the value unquoted in frontmatter. Quotes become part of the subject
  and are rejected.

The subject is finished. Do not look for a better one.

## HOW TO WRITE THE BODY

Omit the body only for a one-line typo, formatting, or comment change whose
subject already states the whole change. Every other commit gets a body.

Choose the structure with this rule, and do not reconsider it:

- Default: one `## Body` item explaining WHY the change was needed and what
  it affects.
- Use `## Body Summary` + `## Body Details` + `## Body Footer` instead, never
  alongside `## Body`, only when the change has migration steps, breaking
  changes, or issue references to record in the footer.

Body text explains rationale and impact. It does not list files or restate
the diff.

## WORKED EXAMPLES
- Bad: `chore: update files` → `feat(mcp): add structured commit retries`,
  with a body explaining what structured retries solve.
- Bad: `fix: stuff` → `fix(parser): preserve prefixed transcript lines`,
  with a body explaining the edge case that dropped them.
- Bad: `refactor: rename files` →
  `refactor(auth): extract token validation to dedicated module`,
  with a body explaining what callers must do differently.

